<p style=3D"line-height:175%"><span style=3D"font-family: Lato, Tahoma, san=
s-serif; font-size: 16px;">For years, SaaS was the safest bet in tech: pred=
ictable revenue, high margins, and pricing based on per-seat subscriptions.=
 But AI agents are starting to break that model. When a single AI can perfo=
rm the work of multiple employees=E2=80=94or even build the software itself=
=E2=80=94the number of =E2=80=9Cseats=E2=80=9D using a product shrinks. Inv=
estors are already seeing the effects.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p style=3D"line-height:175%"><span style=3D"font-family: Lato, Tahoma, san=
s-serif; font-size: 16px;">Companies like Klarna have replaced traditional =
tools such as Salesforce with internal AI systems, and every new AI product=
 launch is sending tremors through SaaS stocks.</span></p>
<p style=3D"line-height:175%"><span style=3D"font-family: Lato, Tahoma, san=
s-serif; font-size: 16px;">The shift goes deeper than just replacing employ=
ees. AI coding agents like Claude Code and Codex are making it dramatically=
 easier for companies to build their own software instead of buying it, put=
ting pressure on SaaS vendors during renewals.&nbsp;</span></p>
<p style=3D"line-height:175%"><span style=3D"font-family: Lato, Tahoma, san=
s-serif; font-size: 16px;">Investors are calling the moment the =E2=80=9CSa=
aSpocalypse,=E2=80=9D but it may be more of a transformation than a collaps=
e. The next generation of software companies is experimenting with usage-ba=
sed or outcome-based pricing, where customers pay for results instead of se=
ats. In other words, software isn=E2=80=99t disappearing=E2=80=94it=E2=80=
=99s just shedding its old business model.</span></p>
